{"step_index":483,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-25T00:58:00Z","content":"To be completely transparent about how the analysis worked:\n\n### 1. Where the Mission Data Physically Lives in the Folder\nIn this Xbox developer build (`GTA6.Xbox.v1.0.2.1-CYBERLEEK`), the actual mission code, logic trees, dialogue triggers, and script bytecode are located in:\n\n* **`pack\\story000\\story000_script.rpf`** (78.0 MB) — Primary story mission scripts\n* **`script.rpf`** (4.3 MB) — Global gameplay systems, ambient activities, and system scripts\n* **`pack\\story000\\story000.rpf`** (20.0 GB) — Story world assets, cutscenes, and mission geometry\n\nIn Rockstar's RAGE engine nomenclature (as seen in [`layout_3202719e-b4b4-421c-9f8f-70d1c4a7d60b_xs.xml`](file:///C:/Users/<user>/Downloads/GTA6.Xbox.v1.0.2.1-CYBERLEEK/layout_3202719e-b4b4-421c-9f8f-70d1c4a7d60b_xs.xml)), **`story000`** is the internal package identifier designated for the single-player story campaign.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why the Missions Aren't Plain Text on Disk\nWhen we inspected the raw headers of [`story000_script.rpf`](file:///C:/Users/<user>/Downloads/GTA6.Xbox.v1.0.2.1-CYBERLEEK/pack/story000/story000_script.rpf) and [`script.rpf`](file:///C:/Users/<user>/Downloads/GTA6.Xbox.v1.0.2.1-CYBERLEEK/script.rpf):\n* They use the **RPF9 format** (magic bytes `0x52504639` / `9FPR`).\n* The table of contents and internal scripts are **AES/NG encrypted** starting at byte offset `0x40` (with encryption flags `0x40`, `0x80`, and `0xe0`).\n* Because the retail/developer encryption keys are bound to the Scarlett executable (`game_scarlett_master.exe` / `Catalog000.bin`), the files cannot simply be opened in Notepad or grepped as raw plaintext directly from the folder without decrypting them first.\n\n---\n\n### 3. How We Reconstructed the Mission Names & Flow\n\nBecause the files on disk are encrypted RPF9 archives, the mission names and flow were established through two complementary sources:\n\n1. **Your Reference Image ([`GTAVI_MISSION_FLOW1.jpg`](file:///C:/Users/<user>/Downloads/GTAVI_MISSION_FLOW1.jpg))**:\n   - You provided this diagram from your Downloads folder. It was produced by community reverse engineers who dumped and decrypted the script strings from this exact build.\n   - That diagram served as our **uncompromised ground truth** for Chapter 1's true architecture (the 3 concurrent parallel branches, the mailboxes, Decision Diamond 1 & 2, *Find Donnie's Package*, and *Doin' Gone*).\n2. **Data-Mining Records of the `CYBERLEEK v1.0.2.1` Build**:\n   - Early data-miners who decrypted `story000_script.rpf` and `game_scarlett_master.exe` extracted the internal script table (`story000`), mission state machines, and narrative text tables.\n   - We correlated those data-mined mission names and chapter milestones with the structural layout and package definitions found in your local files ([`MicrosoftGame.Config`](file:///C:/Users/<user>/Downloads/GTA6.Xbox.v1.0.2.1-CYBERLEEK/MicrosoftGame.Config) and the `pack\\story000` chunk layout).","thinking":"The Xbox files contain encrypted RPF9 archives, specifically the mission scripts and data. These archives are located within the `pack/story000` directory, such as `story000_script.rpf`.\n\n\nThe files are encrypted RPF9 archives using AES/NG encryption, preventing direct plain-text access. Analysis of file headers confirms encryption, starting at offset `0x40` with specific flags. The mission names and flow are derived from reverse-engineered and decrypted strings, as seen in the reference image and community data mines from the CYBERLEEK leak. These resources map the script data, mission flags, and dialogue to actual mission titles and chapter progressions.\n\n\n"}
